  ],
  "text": "Edison publicly electrocuted animals to prove his rival's system was dangerous. He still lost. Both systems delivered power. The argument was about distance. Push current down a wire and some of it is lost as heat, all the way along. The loss depends on current, not voltage. Halve the current and you lose a quarter as much. So send it at enormous voltage and tiny current, then step it back down at the far end. A transformer does that, and a transformer only works on current that keeps changing direction. Direct current does not change direction, so at the time there was no way to step it up. That is the whole reason alternating current won. Not safety, and not showmanship. But here is the twist. The biggest transmission projects being built now are direct current. Modern electronics can convert it efficiently. Edison was not wrong. He was early. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
